From 968c158401b298a9046891c251b6df1a9cebe878 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:36:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10204/11362] Close three holes an independent review found in
 the scope guard

ITEM 5 -- TWO NARROW SCOPES DEADLOCKED EACH OTHER. Every active declaration
judged every commit, so agent A declaring a.py refused agent B's commit of its
OWN declared b.py. Demonstrated in a scratch repo by the reviewer, and it bit
that reviewer live during the session. It was masked only because the sole
standing declaration is '*', which matches everything.

Out-of-scope is now judged against the COMMITTING session's declaration, via
CARNOT_AGENT_SCOPE_ID. Two properties kept deliberately: seals still apply
across ALL declarations, because a harness file is sealed against everyone; and
an unidentified committer is judged by EVERY declaration, which is the strict
direction rather than the convenient one. 4 mutations RED, including one that
makes an unidentified committer answer to nobody.

ITEM 6 -- THE STANDING DECLARATION HAD NO RE-ASSERTION. It lives in gitignored
local state, so a clone, a deleted file, or any agent's `--release` silently
reverted the repo to a fully inert guard with zero detection. A guard that can
be switched off by accident, quietly, is worse than one never installed,
because people believe it is running. The conductor now re-declares it at
startup, idempotently and fail-open -- a conductor that will not boot because a
lint helper moved is a worse outcome than a session without the scope. Proved
end to end: released the scope, watched --list go inert, ran the startup path,
watched it come back identical.

ITEM 4 -- THE SEALED LIST LIED BY OMISSION. An explicit --unseal in ANY
declaration lifts that path for every session, and the conductor's standing
scope unseals four of the nine, so the effective sealed set is FIVE and the
fabrication gate is not among them. That is intended -- sealing a file the loop
edits hourly would wedge it -- but SEALED_PATHS read as nine. Documented at the
tuple, where the misreading happens, rather than in a note nobody opens.

Landed through the guard's own designed route: it refused this commit because
harness_integrity_lint.py is sealed and I was editing it, so I declared a scope
naming it with --unseal. The dogfood worked.

35 tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
